      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.068,
            0.815,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The World of Numbers"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.069,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "67"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s Laws",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " provided the first rigorous framework for arithmetic with signed numbers, establishing rules such as ‛the product of two debts is a fortune’ "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( - \\times - = + )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , as well as the rules for zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.198,
            0.885,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rational Numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Q) are defined as any number that can be expressed as a ratio "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { p } { q }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (where p and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "q"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are integers and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "q \\neq 0 )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Brahmagupta also gave the formal rules for addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division of rational numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.3,
            0.885,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rational numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "dense",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", meaning a rational number always exists between any two other rational numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.349,
            0.887,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Irrational Numbers are values like "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and π that cannot be written as fractions. Their existence proves that the number line contains gaps that rational ratios alone cannot fill. The first proof of the irrationality of a number was that of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , by Hippasus (c. 400 BCE). It was a proof by contradiction. A proof that π is irrational (as it was suspected to be by Āryabhaṭa in 499 CE) was given by the Swiss mathematician Johann Lambert in 1761."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.521,
            0.886,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Real Numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (R) represent the total union of all rational and irrational numbers, forming a perfectly continuous and unbroken line where every real physical measurement has a corresponding point."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.612,
            0.886,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Decimal expansions",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " serve as a mathematical signature for rational vs. irrational: rational numbers always result in terminating or repeating decimals, while irrational numbers produce non-repeating decimals that continue infinitely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.702,
            0.886,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cyclic Numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", such as the digits found in the repeating block of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 7 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (i.e., 142857), reveal the elegant and symmetrical internal patterns hidden within the rational numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.787,
            0.888,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Imaginary Numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are introduced as a final conceptual frontier to handle operations like "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt { - 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , which cannot be solved on the real number line and require a new dimension of mathematics."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
